==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
16th century (as {{m|en|indico}}, modern spelling from the 17th century), {{der|en|es|índigo}}, {{der|en|pt|endego}} (modern {{m|pt|índigo}}), or {{der|en|nl|-}} (via {{der|en|pt|-}}) {{m|nl|indigo}}, all from {{der|en|la|indicum|t=indigo}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἰνδικόν|t=Indian dye}}, from {{m|grc|Ἰνδία}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> {{doublet|en|Indic}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|ĭn'dĭgō|a=UK}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɪn.dɪˌɡəʊ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-indigo.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{enPR|ĭn'dĭgō|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɪndɪˌɡoʊ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|s|indigoes}}

# A purplish-[[blue]] [[colour]].
#: {{color panel|00416A}}
#: {{color panel|web indigo|2=4B0082}}
#* {{RQ:Bindloss Dust of Conflict|chapter=6|passage=The night was considerably clearer than anybody on board her desired when the schooner ''Ventura'' headed for the land. It rose in places, black and sharp against the velvety '''indigo''', over her dipping bow, though most of the low littoral was wrapped in obscurity.}}
# An indigo-colored [[dye]] obtained from certain [[plant]]s ({{vern|indigo plant}} or [[woad]]), or a similar [[synthetic]] dye.
# An indigo plant, such as from species in genera {{taxfmt|Indigofera|genus}}, {{taxfmt|Amorpha|genus}} ({{vern|false indigo}}), {{taxfmt|Baptisia|genus}} ([[wild indigo]]), and {{taxlink|Psorothamnus|genus}} and {{taxfmt|Dalea|genus}} ([[indigo bush]]).

